## Resolution update — 05 October

The [course engineering update](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows)
reports support #4471 closed on 05 October:

- **Parser — fixed:** `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
  [Parser issue](https://github.com/Kialarr/mega-s01e03/issues/1) is closed as completed.
- **Hint — won't fix:** booking-v3 form retired.
  [Hint issue](https://github.com/Kialarr/mega-s01e03/issues/2) is closed as not planned.
  Retirement is not a hint fix and must not be described as one.

These resolutions come from the course update, not tests against the product.
The counts below describe the 04 October export; they are not current queue totals.

## What the export shows

The [incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one queued fix. Its own
cause table disagrees. Treat the table as a classification of the supplied reports,
not proof that the production parser or form has been fixed.

<ExportEvidence />

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

**Parser:** for day-first tenants, the shared parser reads dates month first.
App and email reports, and reports through the day-first booking-v2 form, belong here.
Keep this cause associated with support ticket #4471.

**Hint:** the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` for day-first tenants, while the
form interprets input in the tenant's order. Following the hint reverses the intended
day and month. At export time this needed its own ticket; the shared-parser fix alone
would not resolve it. The later decision was not to fix the hint because booking-v3
was retired. The table's “no ticket of its own” describes the export only.

The one-day-move footnote is not another count to add. Those reports remain in their
computed cause. Their wording deserves investigation, but neither a timezone defect
nor a third cause is established by this export. Preserve the desk notes and ask for
the original input, expected date, actual date, tenant, entry channel and relevant
timezones before reclassifying them.

## Why the merged queue is misleading

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The chart separates receipt from merging. Reports arrived before they were collected
into this ticket; the large merge burst is not the start of the incident. Reports also
continue after that burst. A queue shrinking through merges is not evidence of a fix.
The chart is bounded to the export, so its right edge cannot tell us whether reports
stopped afterwards.

## Proposed replacement for subject-only merging

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) and the
[triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) authorize matching normalized subjects without reading
the body. Their review dates are stale under the knowledge base's own rule. Keep the
accepted record intact; this proposed replacement needs desk approval before it
supersedes that policy.

1. Read the report and identify the tenant, locale, booking form and entry channel.
   The browser's intake tag is a guess, not the tenant's configuration.
2. Compare the underlying cause, not just the subject or the symptom. Different
   subjects can describe the same cause; identical subjects can hide different causes.
3. Merge only when the cause is established and matches. Keep parser and hint reports
   apart, retain report IDs and count each ID once across overlapping export pages.
4. With no tenant or insufficient evidence, leave the report unclassified and investigate.
   “Unknown” is not a shared cause and is not permission to merge.
5. Recheck the live queue before splitting or closing a ticket. Link the replacement
   tickets and updated guidance once the desk approves them; do not invent ticket URLs.

This classifier covers the supplied date incident, not every product the desk supports.
For other symptoms, establish the cause instead of forcing a parser or hint label.

